/*!\page page_sql AnCH SQL library documentation
 *
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * \section sql_intro_seq Introduction
 *
 * AnCH SQL library aims to provide facilities to manage SQL database interactions.
 *
 * \section sql_prereq_sec Prerequisites
 *
 * anch-utils (connection pool), anch-conf (connection factory configuration) and the client library of each
 * driver: libmariadb (or libmysqlclient), libpq and libsqlite3.
 *
 * \section sql_install_sec Installation
 *
 * CMake target \c anch::sql, option \c ANCH_BUILD_SQL ; drivers \c anch::mysql , \c anch::postgresql and \c anch::sqlite are loaded at runtime:
 * \code{.cmake}
find_package(Anch REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my-app PRIVATE anch::sql)
\endcode
 * See \ref install_sec "installation".
 *
 * \section sql_concept_sec Conception
 *
 * AnCH SQL library is a layer to manage SQL client. Managed SQL clients are SQLite3, PostgreSQL and MySQL/MariaDB.\n
 * \n
 * SQl interfaces are inspired by Java JDBC. You will find \ref anch::sql::Connection "Connection", \ref anch::sql::ResultSet "ResultSet",, \ref anch::sql::PreparedStatement "PreparedStatement" and \ref anch::sql::SqlException "SqlException".
 * \ref anch::sql::ResultSet "ResultSet" can be handle with row mapper and complete extract \c std::function "std::function" trough queryMapRow and queryExtract methods.
 * These methods will release \ref anch::sql::ResultSet "ResultSet".\n
 * \n
 * AnCH SQL provides connection factory.
 *
 * \subsection sql_conception_stmt_sec Prepared statements
 *
 * Prepared statements use \c '?' placeholders, numbered from \c 1 . Their number is given by the database (MySQL and
 * SQLite count them on the server side) ; for PostgreSQL, \c '?' outside string literals, quoted identifiers and
 * comments are replaced by \c $n (see \ref anch::sql::findPlaceholders ). PostgreSQL \c '?' operators (\c jsonb )
 * can not be used in prepared statements: use their functions (\c jsonb_exists , ...) instead.\n
 * Every parameter has to be bound before execution ; \c NULL is bound with \c setNull , a \c NULL pointer or an empty
 * \c std::optional .
 *
 * \subsection sql_conception_errors_sec Errors
 *
 * Errors raise \ref anch::sql::SqlException "SqlException" and are reported to the connection, from its prepared
 * statements and result sets too. A fatal error (\c SqlException::isOK is \c false : connection lost or killed,
 * server shutdown, out of memory, protocol error) invalidates the connection. Other errors keep it valid.
 *
 * \subsection sql_conception_transactions_sec Transactions
 *
 * Without \c Connection::startTransaction (or \c begin ), each query is committed by the database (auto-commit
 * mode). A transaction is committed only by \c Connection::commit : a transaction which is not ended when the
 * connection is released (returned to its pool or destroyed) is rolled back, with or without error, so that an
 * application exception between two queries does not commit partial work.
 *
 * \subsection sql_conception_pool_sec SQL connection pool
 *
 * SQL connection pools are configured in configuration file dbCon.conf\n
 * When a connection is returned to its pool, \c Connection::release rolls back its unfinished transaction, then invalid
 * connections are destroyed instead of being reused.
 *
 * \subsection sql_conception_stmt_cache_sec Prepared statements cache
 *
 * Each connection keeps its prepared statements in a bounded cache (\c statement-cache-size key of the connection
 * section, \c 256 by default, \c Connection::setStatementCacheSize ). The least recently used statement is evicted
 * and deallocated on the server side when the cache is full ; a statement is not evicted while one of its result sets
 * is alive. Queries built with literal values (\c IN lists of the builder for example) are distinct cache entries:
 * bind values with \c ? placeholders whenever possible.
 *
 * \section sql_example_sec Example
 *
 * SQL connection example:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-sql/examples/connection.cpp example
 *
 * Pool configuration example:
 * \code{.txt}
[anch::sql.anch_mysql]
driver=MySQL
host=localhost
port=3306
user=anch
password=anch
database=anch_test

[anch::sql.anch_mysql.pool]
max-size=20
init-size=2

[anch::sql.anch_pgsql]
driver=PostgreSQL
host=localhost
port=5432
user=anch
password=anch
database=anch_test
statement-cache-size=512

[anch::sql.anch_pgsql.pool]
max-size=20
init-size=2
timeout=200

[anch::sql.anch_sqlite]
driver=SQLite3
database=anch.db\endcode
 *
 * Pool example:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-sql/examples/pool.cpp example
 */
